<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="policies-and-approvals"
      updated="August 4, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"how-decisions", "How a decision is made"},
        {"first-policy", "A sensible first policy"},
        {"editing", "Editing the policy"},
        {"scoping", "Scope it to a runner or group"},
        {"approvals", "Approvals"},
        {"standing-grants", "Standing grants"},
        {"reason", "The reason requirement"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="policies-and-approvals">
        <:dek>
          Every dispatch — human or LLM — passes through your account's policy before it touches a
          runner. The policy answers one question per action: run it now, stop for a human, or
          refuse.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_h2 id="how-decisions">How a decision is made</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Scope chooses one policy for each dispatch. A runner or group can use a targeted ruleset
        instead of the account default. Two layers then decide in this order:
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-6 text-zinc-300">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-100">Per-action overrides.</strong>
          The policy evaluates an ordered list of action ID patterns and decisions. For example,
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">nomad.job_*</code>
          can require approval and
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">*.delete_*</code>
          can deny. The first matching override wins. Put the most specific patterns first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-100">Risk-tier defaults.</strong>
          If no override matches, the action's declared risk tier
          (<.docs_risk tier="low" /> / <.docs_risk tier="medium" /> / <.docs_risk tier="high" /> /
          <.docs_risk tier="critical" />) picks the decision.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each pack action declares its risk tier. The caller and control plane cannot change it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Policy decisions are <code class="text-brand-300">allow</code>, <code class="text-amber-300">require_approval</code>,
        and <code class="text-red-400">deny</code>. The allow decision dispatches immediately. The
        require_approval decision holds for a person. The deny decision refuses the request. If
        policy cannot answer, it denies the dispatch. This covers missing policies and unknown
        tiers.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="The shipped default.">
        New accounts allow low and medium, require approval for high, and deny critical. There are
        no overrides. This is a conservative first-day policy. Reads and routine diagnostics
        proceed. High-risk actions wait for approval. Critical actions stay blocked until you
        explicitly allow them.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="first-policy">A sensible first policy</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keep the shipped tier defaults, then add overrides only as friction teaches you where
        they belong:
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          Run a week with the defaults. Watch the
          <a
            href={~p"/app/approvals"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Approvals</a>
          queue — the requests
          that keep showing up are your override candidates.
        </li>
        <li>
          Pin down anything you never want an agent to touch
          (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">*.purge_*</code>
          → <code class="text-red-400">deny</code>) — a deny override beats relying on everyone
          remembering.
        </li>
        <li>
          Convert repeated approvals into expiring standing grants, not
          <code class="text-brand-300">allow</code>
          overrides. Grants preserve per-key scope and the audit trail.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An override can also let an agent work without waiting. Some reads are high risk because
        their output can expose secrets. A full config dump can contain a credential in an
        unpredictable directive. The shipped policy therefore holds it for a person. If your fleet
        configurations contain no secrets, you can allow that action. You can also accept the
        exposure to avoid pausing investigations. Record that choice in your policy:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Policy → overrides">overrides:
  - name: <span class="text-brand-300">allow-journal-reads</span>
    action: <span class="text-brand-300">linux.journalctl*</span>
    decision: <span class="text-brand-300">allow</span>
  - name: <span class="text-brand-300">allow-nginx-config-dump</span>
    action: <span class="text-brand-300">nginx.config_dump</span>
    decision: <span class="text-brand-300">allow</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Your policy records that decision. Every completed run records the requesting actor in the
        audit trail. Redaction still masks recognized credential patterns. Pattern-based redaction
        is a backstop, not a guarantee. Treat the output as sensitive when you allow a full dump.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="editing">Editing the policy</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/policies"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Policies</a>
        in the console sidebar. Owners and admins can edit. Operators and viewers see the current
        rules. The editor shows four tier menus above the ordered override list. The editor and API
        require monotonic tier defaults. A higher tier cannot be more permissive than a lower tier.
        This prevents a policy from allowing <.docs_risk tier="critical" />
        actions while requiring approval for <.docs_risk tier="medium" /> actions.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/policy-editor.webp"
        title="emisar console · Policy"
        caption="Risk-tier defaults, the per-action override list, and approval requirements in one editor."
        alt="The Policy page in the emisar console: risk-tier defaults (Low and Medium set to allow, High to require approval, Critical to deny), the per-action override list, and the approval-requirements panel."
      />
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The preview applies the live rules to the selected scope's actual catalog. It shows the
        decision for each action before you save.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A save that changes rules increases the policy version and applies to the next dispatch.
        Every save writes an audit event. Its diff is empty when the rules did not change. Each run
        records the policy version that gated it. An audit review can therefore recover the policy
        in force for that run.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="scoping">Scope it to a runner or group</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The default policy governs the fleet. Add a targeted ruleset when one runner or group needs
        different rules. Examples include a locked database host and a permissive staging group.
        Add the ruleset for that runner or group in <a
          href={~p"/app/policies"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Policies</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The most specific ruleset governs a dispatch. A runner ruleset wins first. Otherwise, its
        group ruleset applies. The default policy applies when neither exists.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A ruleset <strong class="text-zinc-200">replaces</strong>
        the default policy for that runner or group, so what the editor
        shows for a scope is exactly what runs there. Remove a ruleset and that scope falls back
        to the next-broader policy.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:tip} title="Pattern: let an agent iterate on staging, hands-off">
        <p>
          Apply an allow-everything ruleset to the <strong class="text-zinc-200">staging</strong>
          group. Set every tier to
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs text-brand-300">allow</code>
          and require no approvals. An agent can reproduce a bug, test a fix, and verify the result
          without waiting. It can then hand you the production change. Your stricter default still
          requires approval for each production mutation. The blast radius stays in staging. The
          production gate remains.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="approvals">Approvals</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        When policy returns <code class="text-amber-300">require_approval</code>, it creates a held
        run and an approval request. Owners, admins, and operators receive an email with a direct
        link. The request also appears under <strong>Approvals</strong>
        in the console with a live badge.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            One approval releases a run by default. For destructive work, require
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">four-eyes</strong>
            with two approvers and self-approval off. The requester cannot approve that work.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            The approver sees <strong class="text-zinc-200">who asked, the exact action and
            arguments, the target runner, and the stated reason</strong> — enough to decide
            without leaving the page. One click approves or denies.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Undecided requests <strong class="text-zinc-200">expire after 24 hours</strong> and
            the held run is cancelled — nothing waits forever.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            An LLM waiting on an approval long-polls
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">wait_for_run</code>
            and picks
            the result up the moment a human decides — no busy retry loop.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Each vote and final decision is audited with its approver, run ID, decision, and reason.
            Follow the run ID for the requester, action, arguments, and dispatch reason.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="standing-grants">Standing grants</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Approving the same
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">nomad job revert</code>
        for each of 20 allocations creates approval fatigue. When you approve a request, choose its
        duration. Use <strong class="text-zinc-200">once</strong>
        for this run only. You can instead create a standing grant for <strong class="text-zinc-200">1 hour, 24 hours, 30 days, or 90 days</strong>.
        Matching future calls then skip the queue:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Scoped tight.</strong>
            A grant is bound to the API key,
            action, and optional runner. At approval time, bind either the <em>exact</em>
            argument fingerprint or any arguments.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Capped and revocable.</strong>
            Grants can carry a
            maximum use count, expire on schedule, and are listed under
            <a
              href={~p"/app/approvals"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Approvals</a>
            where one click revokes them early. Every use is still a
            fully audited run.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="reason">The reason requirement</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every UI or MCP dispatch must include a <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">reason</code>.
        Approvers see it, and the run and audit event store it. For LLM traffic, the reason
        explains why the model ran <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">nomad job revert</code>.
        The action name alone records only what it ran.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An MCP caller can add two optional fields beyond <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">reason</code>.
        First, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">evidence</code>
        records prior findings or run IDs that informed the call. Second,
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">expected</code>
        records the outcome that confirms success. Together, they preserve what the agent saw, why
        it acted, and what result it expected. Operator and runbook dispatches leave both fields
        empty.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
